Electrical discharge machining is widely used for nickel‑based superalloys such as Hastelloy because these alloys are difficult to machine by conventional methods; in EDM the workpiece material is removed by electrical discharges between the workpiece and a tool electrode. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} The physical and mechanical properties of the electrode therefore directly influence process efficiency, machining accuracy, and the surface roughness of the machined part. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} Electrode selection thus affects both the economic cost (through tool consumption) and the functional quality of the finished surface (through roughness and integrity). {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Electrode wear is a primary concern because it alters electrode geometry, degrades dimensional accuracy, and increases tooling costs. {{cite:S6#c1}} Experimental studies on Hastelloy C‑22 using graphite electrodes report systematic effects of electrode microstructure: coarser‑grained graphite provides higher material removal rates but results in greater relative tool wear, whereas finer‑grained graphite tends to produce lower surface roughness and, in some reports, reduced tool wear and increased process efficiency. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} The mechanism proposed for these observations is that graphite grains can detach from the electrode surface during discharge; detached grains modify discharge stability and thus influence removal efficiency and wear behaviour. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Process parameters mediate these material‑dependent effects. Studies identify peak/discharge current and pulse duration as the dominant drivers of material removal rate and average surface roughness, and the time interval (off‑time) as a dominant factor for relative electrode wear in graphite electrodes on Hastelloy. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}} Published work on other Hastelloy grades also shows that current and pulse duration strongly affect electrode wear patterns and end‑form evolution (for example, transformation of sharp electrode edges into hemispherical shapes due to unstable sparking and heat diffusion). {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}}

Surface integrity after EDM—encompassing surface roughness, recast layer characteristics, and subsurface damage—is the second major concern because it determines component performance in hostile environments. {{cite:S6#c1}} Approaches to improve EDM outcomes reported in the literature include process variants and adjunct techniques such as cryogenic treatment of the workpiece, powder‑mixed dielectric, ultrasonic assistance, and combined process improvements; these are investigated for their effects on MRR, TWR, surface roughness, and recast layer formation. {{cite:S6#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}}

Collectively, the reviewed studies on Hastelloy show consistent links among electrode material and microstructure, machining parameters (notably current and pulse duration), discharge stability, and the twin outcomes of tool wear and surface integrity. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}} Mathematical and empirical models have been developed in some of these studies to predict machining effects and to guide parameter selection for trade‑offs among process efficiency, tool wear rate, and surface roughness. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Electrical discharge machining is widely used for nickel-based superalloys such as Hastelloy because these alloys are difficult to machine by conventional means; in EDM the workpiece material is removed by electrical discharges between the workpiece and the tool electrode, so EDM provides a route to manufacture complex shapes from these materials. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

The choice of electrode material shapes both the cost and the quality of the process because the physical and mechanical properties of the electrode material directly affect process efficiency, machining accuracy, and surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}} Graphite has seen increased use as an electrode material owing to its low purchase cost, good machinability, and high sublimation temperature, properties that influence both economic and technical outcomes. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Electrode wear is one of the main concerns in EDM for nickel-based superalloys because wear alters electrode geometry and therefore accuracy and repeatability; tool wear manifests as changes in electrode shape driven by repeated thermal effects from the spark, with unstable sparks and heat diffusion capable of transforming sharp electrode features into rounded or hemispherical ends. {{cite:S4#c1}} The EDM literature identifies high tool wear rate, together with low material removal rate and high surface roughness, as central challenges and motivates combined process variants—such as cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed EDM, ultrasonic assistance, and others—to mitigate these effects. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Surface integrity after machining is the second concern examined in this review because the electrode material and process parameters determine the machined surface texture and subsurface condition; experimental studies therefore quantify surface roughness (Ra) alongside material removal rate (MRR) and tool wear rate (TWR) to characterise process outcomes. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Empirical studies identify the primary process drivers and show how they interact with electrode material properties: discharge current (peak current) and pulse duration (pulse-on time) are reported as the main factors controlling MRR and Ra in graphite electrodes, with statistical analyses (ANOVA, Taguchi methods) attributing the largest shares of effect to peak current in determining both surface roughness and MRR in superalloy EDM experiments. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}} 

Comparative experiments on graphite electrode grain size report differing but specific effects: one study found that larger-grain graphite (10 µm) provided higher process efficiency (higher MRR) but induced higher relative tool wear, while smaller-grain graphite (1 µm) produced the lowest surface roughness (Ra), and identified discharge current and pulse duration as the dominant parameters for MRR and Ra while the time interval dominated relative tool wear for both graphite grades. {{cite:S1#c1}} Another study by the same group reports that fine-grained graphite electrodes increase process efficiency and reduce tool wear, and further explains that graphite grains detached from the electrode affect the stability of electrical discharges and thus process efficiency. {{cite:S3#c1}} 

These findings link mechanisms to measured outcomes: grain detachment from the electrode alters discharge stability and therefore the material-removal mechanism and efficiency, while current and pulse duration control the thermal energy per discharge that determines both MRR and the surface texture produced. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S1#c1}}

Mobile health applications have been proposed as a low‑cost means to support diabetes self‑management, in the context of a large and growing market of health apps and concern about variability in app quality, usability, and connectivity standards. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}  

Their effect on glycaemic control has been measured in several trials and evaluations with mixed results. A multicentre pragmatic randomized controlled trial of the BlueStar app found no difference between intervention and control arms for the primary outcome of HbA1c at 3 months (adjusted mean difference −0.42, 95% CI −1.05 to 0.21; P=.19). {{cite:S6#c1}}  However, exploratory analyses in the same trial indicated a dose–response relation between app use and glycaemic change: each additional day of app use was associated with a 0.016‑point decrease in 3‑month HbA1c (95% CI −0.03 to −0.003), suggesting that sufficient and sustained engagement may be required to translate app use into clinical benefit. {{cite:S6#c1}}  Smaller pilot work using gamification and automated device connectivity (in adolescents with type 1 diabetes) showed a 50% increase in daily blood glucose measurements (from 2.4 to 3.6 per day, P = .006, n = 12), indicating that specific design features can change self‑monitoring behaviour even when improvements in HbA1c would require longer or larger studies to demonstrate. {{cite:S2#c1}}  Systematic reviews of mobile app interventions for health behaviours report that most included studies (17/23) observed statistically significant behaviour change, but that study quality is variable and risk of bias is present, so conclusions about clinical outcomes remain provisional. {{cite:S4#c1}}  

Engagement tends to fall after the first weeks of use, and this decline helps explain heterogeneous trial results. Reported retention in app interventions varies widely (60%–100% in the review), and qualitative work identifies multiple barriers to continued use—such as perceived time burden, lack of personalised guidance, limited credibility, inadequate goal‑setting or reminders, and concerns about sharing personal information—while motivators include tracking for awareness, personalised feedback, and social or reward mechanisms. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}}  Empirical app‑usage data from trials show large between‑site variation in median days of use over 14 weeks (for example, medians of 36 days [IQR 10.5–124] versus 9 days [IQR 6–51] at different sites), indicating that context and implementation strongly influence sustained engagement. {{cite:S6#c1}}  In combination, these findings point to mechanisms by which apps can influence glycaemic control—principally through self‑monitoring, real‑time feedback, individualised content, clinician involvement, and behaviour‑change techniques—but also to the conditions required for those mechanisms to operate (adequate duration of use, high usability, contextual support, and trustworthy app quality). {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}
